ng prime factorizations. Such functions are typically related to logarithms, but we can still determine their behavior algebraically using the given information.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Use the given values to find $ f(2) $ and $ f(5) $\n\nWe are told:\n\n- $ f(10) = 14 $\n- $ f(40) = 20 $\n\nWe factor the numbers:\n\n- $ 10 = 2 \\times 5 $, so:\n  $$\n  f(10) = f(2) + f(5) = 14 \\quad \\text{(Equation 1)}\n  $$\n\n- $ 40 = 8 \\times 5 = 2^3 \\times 5 $, so:\n  $$\n  f(40) = f(2^3) + f(5) = 3f(2) + f(5) = 20 \\quad \\text{(Equation 2)}\n  $$\n\nWe now have the system of equations:\n\n1. $ f(2) + f(5) = 14 $\n2. $ 3f(2) + f(5) = 20 $\n\nSubtracting Equation 1 from Equation 2:\n\n$$\n(3f(2) + f(5)) - (f(2) + f(5)) = 20 - 14 \\Rightarrow 2f(2) = 6 \\Rightarrow f(2) = 3\n$$\n\nSubstitute back to find $ f(5) $:\n\n$$\nf(2) + f(5) = 14 \\Rightarrow 3 + f(5) = 14 \\Rightarrow f(5) = 11\n$$\n\nSo:\n- $ f(2) = 3 $\n- $ f(5) = 11 $\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Determine $ f(500) $\n\nWe now factor $ 500 $ into primes:\n\n$$\n500 = 2^2 \\times 5^3\n$$\n\nUsing the property $ f(xy) = f(x) + f(y) $, we apply it to the product:\n\n$$\nf(500) = f(2^2) + f(5^3)\n$$\n\nWe use the fact that for any prime power $ p^k $, the function satisfies:\n\n$$\nf(p^k) = k \\cdot f(p)\n$$\n\nSo:\n\n- $ f(2^2) = 2 \\cdot f(2) = 2 \\cdot 3 = 6 $\n- $ f(5^3) = 3 \\cdot f(5) = 3 \\cdot 11 = 33 $\n\nNow sum these:\n\n$$\nf(500) = f(2^2) + f(5^3) = 6 + 33 = 39\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Final Answer\n\n$$\n\\boxed{39}\n$$<|im_end|>'], label: 39, reward: {'score': -1.0, 'acc': False, 'pred': '[INVALID]'}
