[36m(RolloutManager pid=3975)[0m [2026-10-10 16:17:36.514 rollout_manager] fully_async_rollout.py:289 - First rollout sample: ['<|im_start|>user\nSolve the following math problem step by step. The last line of your response should be of the form Answer: $Answer (without quotes) where $Answer is the answer to the problem.\n\nCompute the remainder when $98!$ is divided by $101$.\n\nRemember to put your answer on its own line after "Answer:".<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>assistant\n<think>\nOkay, so I need to find the remainder when 98! is divided by 101. Hmm, 101 is a prime number, right? Let me check that first. I remember that primes are numbers greater than 1 that have no divisors other than 1 and themselves. 101 is indeed a prime number because it\'s not divisible by any number up to its square root, which is around 10. So, 101 is prime. \n\nNow, I recall something about Wilson\'s theorem. Wilson\'s theorem states that for a prime number p, (p-1)! ≡ -1 mod p. So, if p is prime, then (p-1)! + 1 is divisible by p. Let me verify that. For example, if p is 5, then 4! = 24, and 24 + 1 = 25, which is 5 squared, so yes, 24 ≡ -1 mod 5. That seems right. So applying Wilson\'s theorem here, since 101 is prime, then (101-1)! ≡ -1 mod 101, which is 100! ≡ -1 mod 101. \n\nBut the problem is asking for 98! mod 101. So how does 98! relate to 100!?\n\nWell, 100! can be written as 100 × 99 × 98!. So, substituting that into Wilson\'s theorem, we have:\n\n100 × 99 × 98! ≡ -1 mod 101.\n\nSo, I need to find the value of 98! modulo 101. Let me write that equation again:\n\n100 × 99 × 98! ≡ -1 mod 101.\n\nTherefore, if I can compute 100 × 99 mod 101, then I can solve for 98! mod 101.\n\nFirst, let\'s compute 100 mod 101. Since 100 is 101 - 1, so 100 ≡ -1 mod 101. Similarly, 99 is 99 mod 101, which is just 99.\n\nSo substituting these into the equation:\n\n(-1) × 99 × 98! ≡ -1 mod 101.\n\nLet me rearrange this equation. Multiply both sides by -1:\n\n99 × 98! ≡ 1 mod 101.\n\nSo now we have 99