mod 101 is 51, so -51 mod 101 is 50. Yep, same result. So that\'s consistent.\n\nTherefore, 98! ≡ 50 mod 101.\n\nSo the remainder is 50.\n\n**Final Answer**\nAnswer: 50\n</think>\n\nTo solve the problem of finding the remainder when $ 98! $ is divided by $ 101 $, we can use **Wilson\'s Theorem**.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Understanding the Problem\n\nWilson\'s Theorem states that for a prime number $ p $, we have:\n\n$$\n(p - 1)! \\equiv -1 \\pmod{p}\n$$\n\nSince $ 101 $ is a prime number, we can apply this theorem:\n\n$$\n100! \\equiv -1 \\pmod{101}\n$$\n\nOur goal is to find $ 98! \\mod 101 $.\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Express $ 100! $ in Terms of $ 98! $\n\nWe can write:\n\n$$\n100! = 100 \\times 99 \\times 98!\n$$\n\nSubstitute into Wilson\'s Theorem:\n\n$$\n(100 \\times 99 \\times 98!) \\equiv -1 \\pmod{101}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Simplify the Expression Modulo 101\n\nWe compute each term modulo 101:\n\n- $ 100 \\equiv -1 \\pmod{101} $\n- $ 99 \\equiv -2 \\pmod{101} $\n\nSo the equation becomes:\n\n$$\n(-1) \\times (-2) \\times 98! \\equiv -1 \\pmod{101}\n$$\n\n$$\n2 \\times 98! \\equiv -1 \\pmod{101}\n$$\n\nMultiply both sides by the modular inverse of 2 modulo 101. The inverse of 2 modulo 101 is 51 (since $ 2 \\times 51 = 102 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{101} $):\n\n$$\n98! \\equiv -1 \\times 51 \\pmod{101}\n$$\n\n$$\n98! \\equiv -51 \\pmod{101}\n$$\n\nSince $ -51 \\equiv 50 \\pmod{101} $, we have:\n\n$$\n98! \\equiv 50 \\pmod{101}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Final Answer\n\nAnswer: $ \\boxed{50} $<|im_end|>'], label: 50, reward: {'score': 1.0, 'acc': True, 'pred': '50'}
