ybe manipulate the equation. Let me write it as:\n\n(2/3)^k = 2\n\nLet me take natural logarithm on both sides:\n\nk * ln(2/3) = ln(2)\n\nTherefore, k = ln(2) / ln(2/3)\n\nAlternatively, I can write this as k = ln(2) / [ln 2 - ln 3] because ln(2/3) = ln 2 - ln 3.\n\nBut maybe there\'s another way. Let me see. Alternatively, maybe express 2 as (2/3)^(something). Wait, 2 = (2/3)^(-k) ? Wait, since (2/3)^k = 2, then (2/3)^k = 2, so (2/3)^k = 2. Let me write 2 as (2/3)^{something}. Let me think.\n\nAlternatively, maybe express 2 as 2/1. Let me try to write both sides with exponents.\n\nAlternatively, maybe express 2 as (2/3)^{-1} * 3^{-1}? Not sure. Maybe it\'s easier to just keep it as k = ln(2)/ln(2/3). \n\nBut maybe I can express this in terms of log base 2/3 or something. Wait, let me recall that log_b a = ln a / ln b. So, if we take k = log_{2/3} 2. Because log_{2/3} 2 = ln 2 / ln (2/3) = same as above. So, k = log_{2/3} 2.\n\nBut maybe I can use this value of k to find x. Let\'s go back to the original equations:\n\nFrom equation 1: (3x)^k = 4\n\nFrom equation 2: (2x)^k = 8\n\nBut if I can find k, then I can plug it into either equation to solve for x.\n\nAlternatively, since we have two equations, maybe we can express x from one equation and plug into another.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps I can take the ratio of equations again.\n\nBut let\'s see. Let me try plugging k into equation 1:\n\n(3x)^k = 4.\n\nBut k = log_{2/3} 2. Therefore:\n\n(3x)^{log_{2/3} 2} = 4.\n\nHmm, this seems complicated. Maybe another approach. Let me think.\n\nAlternatively, since both equations involve x, maybe we can take the ratio of the two equations.\n\nWait, we already did that. So, from (2x)^k = 8 and (3x)^k = 4, then dividing gives (2/3)^k = 2, so k = log_{2/3} 2. Then, maybe I can substitute back into one of the original equations.\n\nLet me take equation 2: (2x)^k = 8. So, substituting k:\n\n(2x)^{log_{2/3} 2} = 8.\n\nThis seems complicated. Maybe take logarithms again? Let me take natural logs:\n\nln( (2x)^{log_{2/3} 2} ) = ln 8\n\nWhich is log_{2/3} 2 * ln(2x) = ln 8\n\nBut log_{2/3} 2 is equal to ln 2 / ln(2/3). So:\n\n[ln 2 / ln(2/3)] * ln(2x) = ln 8\n\nHmm, this seems messy. Maybe there\'s a better way.\n\nWait, perhaps instead of introducing k, I can express both equations in terms of logarithms with the same base?\n\nAlternatively, use change of base formula. Let me think.\n\nLet me recall that log_b a = ln a / ln b. So, let\'s rewrite both sides as:\n\nlog_{3x} 4 = ln 4 / ln (3x)\n\nlog_{2x} 8 = ln 8 / ln (2x)\n\nTherefore, the equation is:\n\nln 4 / ln (3x) = ln 8 / ln (2x)\n\nCross multiplying:\n\nln 4 * ln (2x) = ln 8 * ln (3x)\n\nHmm, this seems a bit complex, but maybe we can simplify.\n\nFirst, note that ln 4 is 2 ln 2, and ln 8 is 3 ln 2. So substituting:\n\n2 ln 2 * ln (2x) = 3 ln 2 * ln (3x)\n\nDivide both sides by ln 2 (since ln 2 is not zero):\n\n2 * ln (2x) = 3 * ln (3x)\n\nSo:\n\n2 ln(2x) - 3 ln(3x) = 0\n\nLet me write that as:\n\n2 ln(2x) = 3 ln(3x)\n\nDivide both sides by ln(2x) ? Wait, maybe bring all terms to one side:\n\n2 ln(2x) - 3 ln(3x) = 0\n\nAlternatively, use logarithm properties. Let me recall that n ln a = ln(a^n). So:\n\n2 ln(2x) = 3 ln(3x)\n\nWhich can be written as:\n\nln( (2x)^2 ) = ln( (3x)^3 )\n\nTherefore, since the logs are equal, their arguments must be equal:\n\n(2x)^2 = (3x)^3\n\nSo:\n\n4x^2 = 27x^3\n\nDivide both sides by x^2 (since x is a positive real number, x ≠ 0):\n\n4 = 27x\n\nTherefore, x = 4 / 27\n\nWait, 4 divided by 27? Let me check that again.\n\nYes, 4x^2 = 27x^3, so 4 = 27x, so x = 4/27. So, x is 4/27. \n\nBut let me check if this is correct. Let me verify with the original equation.\n\nLet me compute log_{3x} 4 and log_{2x} 8 when x = 4/27.\n\nFirst, compute 3x: 3*(4/27) = 12/27 = 4/9.\n\nSimilarly, 2x: 2*(4/27) = 8/27.\n\nSo the equation becomes:\n\nlog_{4/9} 4 = log_{8/27} 8\n\nLet me check if these are equal.\n\nFirst, compute log base 4/9 of 4. Let me recall that log_b a = ln a / 